ings.", "Incumbent April McClain Delaney (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent April McClain Delaney enters the general election after defeating David Trone and six rivals in an expensive Democratic primary, and she carries a large financial advantage into the fall. She has raised over $12 million and holds outside-group backing, while Republican nominee Robin Ficker, a perennial candidate, has raised about $400,000 and reported only a few hundred dollars on hand. A Green Party candidate is also on the ballot.\n\nThe district leans Democratic by three points on partisanship, and the national environment favors Democrats this cycle. Prediction markets give the incumbent roughly a 94% chance of winning. With no general-election polling available, the forecast rests on fundamentals, which point to a comfortable double-digit Democratic win, though the incumbent's modest cash position and the lack of polling leave some room for uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9849, "margin": 15.18}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9796, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9852, "margin": 17.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9849, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9795, "margin": 17.0}]}